"""English — details, quran_b_2 (35 questions)."""

D = {
"quran_116": "The verse closes with the reason: \"Indeed, Allah is Exalted in Might and Wise\" (31:27). It follows the statement that to Allah belongs whatever is in the heavens and the earth and that He is free of need (31:26), and is followed by the reminder that creating and raising all people is, for Him, like a single soul (31:28). Pens, ink and seven more seas, and still the writing never ends. A Muslim takes from it that the words and knowledge of Allah have no limit.",
"quran_117": "The verse gives the objection in full: \"Why are its verses not explained in detail? Is it a foreign recitation and an Arab messenger?\" It then answers: \"Say: it is, for those who believe, a guidance and a cure\" (41:44). Just before, the surah calls the Qur'an a mighty Book which falsehood cannot approach from before it or behind it (41:41-42). A Muslim takes from it that the Qur'an came in the language of its first hearers so that no one could call it unclear.",
"quran_118": "The verse belongs to the opening of Surah Al-Isra, after the Night Journey (17:1) and the Book given to Musa (17:2). It continues with the other side of the good news: \"and that those who do not believe in the Hereafter, We have prepared for them a painful punishment\" (17:9-10). The next verse observes that man prays for evil as he prays for good, for man is ever hasty (17:11). A Muslim takes from it that the Qur'an points him to the most upright way.",
"quran_119": "The verse addresses all people: \"O mankind\" (10:57). Four gifts are named in order, and the last two, guidance and mercy, are given to the believers. The next verse tells them how to receive it: \"Say: in the bounty of Allah and in His mercy, in that let them rejoice; it is better than what they gather\" (10:58). A Muslim takes from it that the healing of the heart comes from what Allah sent down, and that this is a reason for joy.",
"quran_120": "The verse continues with the duty: \"So whoever witnesses the month, let him fast it; and whoever is ill or on a journey, then an equal number of other days. Allah intends for you ease and does not intend for you hardship\" (2:185). The month is honoured for the Qur'an that came down in it, and the Qur'an by three names: guidance, clear proofs, and the criterion. A Muslim takes from it that Ramadan is first the month of the Qur'an, and that its fast is meant to be easy.",
"quran_121": "The oath opens the surah and the people's reaction follows: \"But they wonder that a warner has come to them from among themselves, and the disbelievers say: this is an amazing thing. When we have died and become dust? That is a distant return\" (50:1-3). Allah answers that He knows what the earth takes away of them, and that with Him is a preserving record (50:4). A Muslim takes from it that the return to Allah is as certain as the Book He swears by.",
"quran_122": "The oath is the first of a series that opens the surah, and the series ends with what it is sworn for: \"indeed, what you are promised is to occur\" (77:1-7). The surah then describes that promise: the stars effaced, the sky split, the mountains blown away, the messengers gathered (77:8-13). From there one line returns repeatedly: \"Woe, that Day, to the deniers\" (77:15). A Muslim takes from it that Allah swears by what He sends forth to make the promised Day certain.",
"quran_123": "The passage sets 'Illiyyun against its opposite: earlier in the surah, the record of the wicked is placed in Sijjin (83:7-9). Of the righteous it says: \"And what can make you know what is 'Illiyyun? A register inscribed, which those brought near witness\" (83:18-21). The following verses show the righteous on couches, given a sealed drink mixed from Tasnim (83:22-28). A Muslim takes from it that every deed is written down, and that the record of the good is kept high, witnessed by the nearest angels.",
"quran_124": "The earth follows: \"And when the earth is stretched out and casts out what is within it and becomes empty, and listens to its Lord, as it must\" (84:1-5). Then it addresses the listener: \"O mankind, you are labouring toward your Lord and will meet Him\" (84:6). Two records follow, one given in the right hand, judged with an easy reckoning, and one given behind the back (84:7-12). A Muslim takes from it that the Day begins with creation submitting, and ends with each person holding his own record.",
"quran_125": "The surah opens with a chain of events: the sun wrapped up, the stars scattered, the souls paired, the girl buried alive asked for what sin she was killed, the pages spread open, the sky stripped away, Hellfire kindled and Paradise brought near (81:1-13). All of it leads to one sentence: \"a soul will know what it has brought\" (81:14). A Muslim takes from it that the world he knows will be undone, and that the one thing left standing will be what he did.",
"quran_126": "The verse goes on: \"It will have what it has earned, and it will bear what it has earned\" (2:286). Then it becomes a prayer in the believers' own voice: they ask their Lord not to hold them to account if they forget or err, and not to load them with what they cannot bear; they ask for pardon, forgiveness and mercy. A Muslim takes from it that what Allah asks of him fits his strength, and that he may ask for mercy in the Qur'an's own words.",
"quran_127": "The verse ends: \"And Allah multiplies for whom He wills, and Allah is all-Encompassing, all-Knowing\" (2:261). Seven hundred is not the ceiling. The next verses add a condition: those who spend and then do not follow their gift with reminders or injury have their reward with their Lord (2:262), and \"kind speech and forgiveness are better than charity followed by injury\" (2:263). A Muslim takes from it that a gift for Allah is never lost, and that it must be left in the ground, not dug up by reproach.",
"quran_128": "The verse opens with the address: \"O you who believe, do not nullify your charities with reminders and injury, like one who spends his wealth to be seen by people\" (2:264). The next verse gives the opposite: those who spend seeking Allah's approval are like a garden on high ground, yielding double when rain falls, and still bearing fruit from a drizzle (2:265). A Muslim takes from it that a gift given to be seen, or thrown back at its receiver, leaves nothing behind.",
"quran_129": "The verse continues: \"but he finds Allah before him, and He pays him his account in full; and Allah is swift in reckoning\" (24:39). The next verse gives a second image: darkness in a deep sea, so that a man can hardly see his own hand, \"and he to whom Allah has not given light has no light\" (24:40). Both follow the Verse of Light (24:35). A Muslim takes from it that deeds without faith look full from afar and prove empty when needed.",
"quran_130": "The answer comes in the next verse: \"And We have certainly tried those before them, and Allah will surely make known those who are truthful, and He will surely make known the liars\" (29:1-3). The surah then shows the trial: the one who says he believes but gives up when hurt for the sake of Allah (29:10). A Muslim takes from it that saying \"we believe\" is the beginning of a test, and that the test is what shows the word to be true.",
"quran_131": "The verse ends: \"He increases in creation what He wills; indeed, Allah is over all things able\" (35:1). The wings are not a limit, for Allah adds to His creation as He wills. The next verse describes His hold over what reaches people: whatever mercy Allah opens for them, none can withhold, and whatever He withholds, none can release after Him (35:2). A Muslim takes from it that the angels are His servants and couriers, created with wings as He chose, and that all mercy passes through His hand.",
"quran_132": "The verse ends: \"Indeed, in that are signs for a people who reflect\" (30:21). It stands in a run of signs that opens with man created from dust and spread across the earth (30:20), and continues with the creation of the heavens and the earth and the differences of languages and colours (30:22). The bond between husband and wife is placed among the sky and the earth. A Muslim takes from it that the affection and mercy in a marriage are gifts placed there by Allah.",
"quran_133": "The verse ends: \"Indeed, in that are signs for those of knowledge\" (30:22). It comes after the sign of spouses and the affection and mercy placed between them (30:21), and before the sign of sleep by night and day and the seeking of Allah's bounty (30:23). The verse pairs the vastest thing with the most ordinary. A Muslim takes from it that the many tongues and colours of humankind are a mark of Allah's creation, to be met with knowledge rather than contempt.",
"quran_134": "The statement ends a question put to those who disbelieve: \"Have those who disbelieved not seen that the heavens and the earth were joined together, and We separated them, and We made from water every living thing? Then will they not believe?\" (21:30). The next verses continue: mountains set firm so that the earth does not shake (21:31), and the sky made a protected ceiling (21:32). A Muslim takes from it that the water he drinks is itself a sign, from which Allah made every living thing.",
"quran_135": "The verse ends: \"So exalted is Allah, Lord of the Throne, above what they describe\" (21:22). The order of the sky and the earth is itself the proof: two rulers would pull the creation apart, and the creation holds together. The surah then states that every messenger before Muhammad ﷺ was sent with the same word, that there is no god but Allah, so worship Him (21:25). A Muslim takes from it that the oneness of Allah can be seen in the steadiness of the world.",
"quran_136": "The verse also names what is forbidden: \"and He forbids immorality and bad conduct and oppression; He admonishes you so that you may take heed\" (16:90). The verse that follows keeps the same tone: \"And fulfil the covenant of Allah when you have made it, and do not break oaths after confirming them\" (16:91). A Muslim takes from it a short list of what to do and what to avoid, ordered from the fair to the generous, and from the stranger to his own kin.",
"quran_137": "The verse before it tells how the bee came to its work: \"And your Lord inspired the bee: take for yourself houses among the mountains and the trees and in what they build\" (16:68). The drink of varied colours is the honey, and the verse ends: \"Indeed, in that is a sign for a people who reflect\" (16:69). A Muslim takes from it that the small creature was taught its craft by its Lord, and that its honey is both a healing and a sign.",
"quran_138": "The surah opens with the name of Allah the Most Merciful and lists His gifts: the Qur'an taught, man created, and speech taught to him (55:1-4). The next verses continue: \"the sun and the moon move by precise calculation, and the stars and the trees prostrate\" (55:5-6). A question recurs through the surah: \"So which of the favours of your Lord will you both deny?\" (55:13). A Muslim takes from it that speech, and the Book, are the first favours he is asked to acknowledge.",
"quran_139": "The three groups appear after the earth is shaken and the mountains crumbled to scattered dust (56:4-6). Two are named with a question: \"the companions of the right, what are the companions of the right? And the companions of the left, what are the companions of the left?\" (56:7-10). The third is described differently: \"And the forerunners, the forerunners: those are the ones brought near, in Gardens of Pleasure\" (56:10-12). A Muslim takes from it that the Day sorts people by what they raced toward in this life.",
"quran_140": "The verse ends: \"Wretched is the example of the people who denied the signs of Allah, and Allah does not guide the wrongdoing people\" (62:5). The verse comes after the Messenger ﷺ is described as sent to the unlettered to recite the verses, purify them and teach them the Book (62:2), and before the call to hasten to the remembrance of Allah on the day of Jumu'ah (62:9). A Muslim takes from it that a scripture is honoured not by being carried but by being lived.",
"quran_141": "Her prayer continues: \"and save me from Pharaoh and his deeds, and save me from the wrongdoing people\" (66:11). She asked for a house near Allah while living in the house of Pharaoh. The verse before hers gives the opposite example, the wives of Nuh and Lut, who betrayed the two righteous servants they were married to (66:10); the verse after presents Maryam, daughter of 'Imran (66:12). A Muslim takes from it that faith belongs to the person, not to the household.",
"quran_142": "The surah is four short verses: \"Say: He is Allah, One. Allah, the Eternal Refuge. He neither begets nor is born, nor is there to Him any equal\" (112:1-4). Every line speaks of Allah alone, hence its second name. It begins with the command \"Say\", so the words are given to the Prophet ﷺ as an answer to anyone who asks about his Lord. A Muslim takes from it the shortest complete statement of what he believes about Allah, learned early and repeated often.",
"quran_143": "Two verses complete it: \"Allah, the Eternal Refuge\" before it (112:2), and \"nor is there to Him any equal\" after it (112:4). Begetting and being born belong to creatures; the verse removes both from Allah. The Qur'an speaks elsewhere with the same firmness: \"And they say: the Most Merciful has taken a son. You have brought a monstrous thing\" (19:88-89). Whoever recites Surah Al-Ikhlas states this denial in a single line (112:3). A Muslim takes from it that Allah has no parent and no child.",
"quran_144": "The surah opens with the One in whom refuge is sought, named three times: \"Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of mankind, the King of mankind, the God of mankind\" (114:1-3). Only then comes the evil: the whisperer who retreats, who whispers in the breasts of people, from among the jinn and mankind (114:4-6). Three names of the Protector stand against one enemy. A Muslim takes from it that the whisper in the heart is met by turning to the Lord of all people.",
"quran_145": "The surah opens with the one in whom refuge is taken: \"Say: I seek refuge in the Lord of daybreak\" (113:1), and then comes the list of evils (113:2-5). The three named evils move from what cannot be seen coming, the night, to what people do in secret, the blowing on knots, to what a person feels within, envy. A Muslim takes from it that he does not face the hidden harms of night, sorcery or envy alone, but under the Lord who brings the dawn.",
"quran_146": "The first four verses of the surah speak about Allah: all praise is His, Lord of the worlds, Master of the Day of Recompense (1:1-4). Then the speaker turns to Him directly: \"You alone we worship, and You alone we ask for help\" (1:5). \"You alone\" comes before the verb in both halves, so the exclusiveness is said before the act. A Muslim takes from it that he serves no one but Allah and leans on no one but Allah, and says so in every prayer.",
"quran_147": "The request is spelled out in full: \"Guide us to the straight path, the path of those upon whom You have bestowed favour, not of those who have earned anger, nor of those who are astray\" (1:6-7). The Qur'an names the favoured in another place: \"those whom Allah has favoured of the prophets, the steadfast affirmers of truth, the martyrs and the righteous\" (4:69). A Muslim takes from it that every prayer opens with a plea to be kept on the road walked by the prophets.",
"quran_148": "The verse describes two ways of reading: \"As for those in whose hearts is deviation, they follow what is unspecific of it, seeking discord\" (3:7). Against them stand those firm in knowledge, who say: \"We believe in it; all of it is from our Lord\" (3:7). The next verse gives their prayer: \"Our Lord, let not our hearts deviate after You have guided us\" (3:8). A Muslim takes from it that the precise verses are his foundation, and that an unclear verse calls for trust, not dispute.",
"quran_149": "The verse ends with a question: \"Do you not know that Allah is over all things able?\" (2:106), and the next continues: \"Do you not know that to Allah belongs the dominion of the heavens and the earth, and that you have, besides Allah, no protector and no helper?\" (2:107). Surah An-Nahl records the reaction: when a verse is put in place of another, they say \"you are only an inventor\" (16:101). A Muslim takes from it that whatever Allah changes, He replaces with something equal or better.",
"quran_150": "The complaint (25:30) is answered in the next verse: \"And thus have We made for every prophet an enemy from among the criminals; but sufficient is your Lord as a guide and a helper\" (25:31). The surah then records another objection, \"Why was the Qur'an not sent down to him all at once?\", and answers that it was sent in parts to strengthen the Prophet's ﷺ heart (25:32). A Muslim takes from it that leaving the Qur'an unheeded is a grievance the Messenger ﷺ raises before Allah.",
}
